Q: Updating Multiple Values with Dict in SQLite (Using Python) A: <pre><code>kv = {&quot;column_name&quot;: &quot;new value&quot;}

placeholders = [f&quot;{key} = :{key}&quot; for key in kv.keys()]
attr_statement = &quot;,&quot;.join(placeholders)
query = f&quot;UPDATE tweets SET {attr_statement} WHERE id={id}&quot;
cursor.execute(query, kv)
</code></pre>

Q: Creating relative address references - Rust A: <p>The simplest way to construct a pointer with an arbitrary address is via the <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/ptr/fn.with_exposed_provenance.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>core::ptr::with_exposed_provenance</code></a> function, which explicitly guarantees appropriate provenance for any address <strong>if</strong> that address does not overlap with that of any compiler-allocated object:</p>
<blockquote>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>pub const fn with_exposed_provenance&lt;T&gt;(addr: usize) -&gt; *const T;
</code></pre>
<p><em>Converts an address back to a pointer, picking up some previously ‘exposed’ provenance.</em></p>
<p><em>... memory which is outside the control of the Rust abstract machine (MMIO registers, for example) is always considered to be accessible with an exposed provenance, so long as this memory is disjoint from memory that will be used by the abstract machine such as the stack, heap, and statics.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>(Informally, provenance is the compiler's way of keeping track of which pointers can access which allocations. It is used to avoid extraneous memory accesses to objects that don't mutate, but since the Rust compiler can't keep track of the entire memory map, some pointers are allowed have extra leverage.)</p>
<p>Thus:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>use core::ffi::c_int; // The `int` data type from C.
use core::ptr;

// Construct the pointer with the specific address:
let value: *const c_int = ptr::with_exposed_provenance(0x0061FF0C);

// Read the value at the address.
//
// SAFETY: OP guarantees that this address is out-
// side of Rust's memory and is supported by the
// underlying hardware. We use an unaligned read as
// we don't necessarily know the alignment of
// `c_int`.
let _: c_int = unsafe { value.read_unaligned() };
</code></pre>
<p>Do note that, in the case of I/O registers, it may be more desirable to instead do volatile accesses via the <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/primitive.pointer.html#method.read_volatile" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;*const _&gt;::read_volatile</code></a> and <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/primitive.pointer.html#method.write_volatile" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;*mut _&gt;::write_volatile</code></a> methods so as to avoid reordering and elision.</p>
